    <string name="trebuchet_tip_beam">A long arm whips faster, but hauls its own weight into the air — past a point the beam eats what the counterweight gives. The collar at the axle trades force for speed.</string>
    <string name="trebuchet_tip_post">A tall post lets the arm plunge deeper, so the counterweight falls from higher: more energy stored, and more travel to give it back.</string>
    <string name="trebuchet_tip_weight">What counts is the ratio to the stone — real machines ran a hundred to four hundred times it. The strap sets the timing: too short and the weight is as good as bolted on, too long and it pushes late.</string>
    <string name="trebuchet_tip_pin">Laid flat, the hook holds the loop to the end of the whip and the shot goes out taut. Stood up, it lets go early: the shot climbs, then goes backwards.</string>
    <string name="trebuchet_tip_sling">A long sling adds radius, so speed — but it takes longer to come round. Past a point the arm is done before the sling is.</string>
